  "guides/bathroom-safety-checklist-older-adults": {
    "file": "Bathroom Safety Checklist.dc.html",
    "type": "guide",
    "eyebrow": "Safety checklist",
    "image": "bathroom-safety",
    "imageAlt": "Well-lit bathroom with clear floor space and safety features",
    "title": "Bathroom Safety Checklist for Older Adults",
    "description": "Use this practical bathroom safety checklist to review floors, lighting, showers, bathtubs, toilets, sinks, storage, accessibility, and emergency planning.",
    "blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Bathrooms combine water, hard surfaces, confined spaces, transfers, changing positions, and frequently used fixtures — so a useful safety review examines the entire room rather than one product. Common improvements include reducing tripping and slipping hazards, improving lighting, adding properly installed support, making bathing and toilet areas easier to use, keeping frequently used items within practical reach, and planning for changing household needs."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "This checklist offers general educational guidance grounded in CDC and National Institute on Aging home-safety materials — not an individual medical or occupational assessment."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Start with the person, not the product"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Before buying equipment, consider current mobility, balance, strength, vision, transfers, use of a cane, walker, or wheelchair, whether assistance is needed, whether needs are changing, and which activities currently feel difficult."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "A feature that works well for one person may be unsuitable for another. Complex mobility, transfer, cognitive, or caregiver needs may benefit from an assessment by an occupational therapist or another qualified professional."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Walk through the bathroom and check each statement that is already true for your home. Whatever stays unchecked becomes your improvement list — start with the unchecked items that affect daily routines most. Your checks save in this browser, and you can print the list instead. Free, no email or account required."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The 2010 ADA Standards for Accessible Design describe recognized accessible-design concepts — roll-in and transfer shower layouts, shower seats, grab-bar positioning, reachable controls, handheld shower units, thresholds, and clearances. ADA requirements generally apply to covered public, commercial, and certain residential facilities: they are useful design references, but an ordinary private home is not automatically required to follow every ADA specification. Grab-bar placement in a home should reflect how the specific person enters, exits, sits, stands, and transfers."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Finding the bathing area is the hardest part? Compare conversions , walk-in showers , and walk-in tubs — or start with the comparison guide ."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "A five-minute bathroom review"
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "When a larger remodel may be appropriate"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "A broader project may deserve consideration when multiple fixtures are difficult to use, the doorway or layout restricts movement, wheelchair access is needed, caregiver assistance is required, drainage or water damage already requires construction, a focused product would not solve the central problem, or household needs are expected to change. Start with the accessible bathroom remodel guide , or the focused paths: walk-in showers and tub-to-shower conversions ."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Who may help assess the bathroom?"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Depending on the situation: an occupational therapist (particularly helpful for evaluating how a specific person performs transfers and daily activities), an accessibility-experienced contractor , a certified aging-in-place professional , a physical therapist , a designer , a plumber , or an electrician . Not all are required for every bathroom, and they play different roles — a simple hazard cleanup needs none of them, while a wheelchair-focused redesign benefits from several."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Frequently asked questions"
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Does Your Bathroom Need More Than Small Safety Updates?"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Explore bathing and remodeling options when the current layout, bathtub, shower, or doorway remains difficult to use."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Recommendations not tied to a cited source are general planning considerations or questions to discuss with a qualified professional. This guide is not medical advice. See our Editorial Policy ."
      }
    ],
    "sources": []
  },
